    "current_example": {
      "math_academy_id": "e-3575",
      "problem": "The graph below shows $y=\\tan(Bx).$ What is the value of $B?$\n\n \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14254788/assets/17fc49acab5835cee89ed7f25bc147941f38cb1e56fe31f61afdda19e50036c8.png)",
      "worked_solution": "In order to find the value of $B,$ we first need to find the period of the function.\n\nFrom the graph, we can see that function makes $3$ complete cycles on the interval $(-\\pi,\\pi).$ So, the period is $\\dfrac{2\\pi}{3}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14254788/assets/9e5e68cd9b576f73c82d8f062c79258bf64c493b2db3f09ec6573cfda9cbd648.png)\n\nWe know that for a function $y=\\tan (Bx),$ the horizontal scaling factor is $\\dfrac{1}{B},$ and the period is multiplied by the scaling factor:\n\n$$\n\\text{period} = \\pi \\cdot \\dfrac{1}{B}\n$$\n\nFor our particular function, the period is $\\dfrac{2\\pi}{3},$ so we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2\\pi }{3} & =\\pi \\cdot \\frac{1}{B} \\\\ \\frac{2}{3} & =\\frac{1}{B} \\\\ B & =\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
